/**
 * test_schedprune.php - email-only scheduler options stay off non-EMAIL tasks.
 *
 * WHY THIS SUITE EXISTS (2026-09-18). The scheduler form posts ONE options object
 * for every task type (main.js: "include both email and snapshot options ...
 * unused fields are harmless"), and a new draft's hidden Recipients field starts
 * out holding its creator. manage_schedule.php stored both wholesale, so every
 * DB_DUMP task carried attach_csv, body_preamble, theme_override ... plus
 * RECIPIENTS = its creator and OPTIONS.recipient_vars = the creator's name, OUC,
 * department and employee id - and every SCHEDULE_* audit snapshot copied it
 * (live 2026-09-18: all 4 DB_DUMP tasks, 42 lifecycle audit rows). Two earlier
 * fixes had patched READERS of the leak (main.js + penumbra_repository.js both
 * guard subscriptions_enabled with "non-EMAIL tasks can carry a stale true").
 * penumbra_sched_prune_email_only() now fixes the WRITER, on both write paths:
 * action=save and action=restore_from_audit.
 *
 * WHAT IS UNDER TEST - three parts, all against the real files:
 *   A. A DRIFT GUARD over schedule_runner.php. Dropping a key is only safe while
 *      no non-EMAIL path reads it. Every string literal naming a pruned key must
 *      sit inside the EMAIL branch, or inside a function whose every call chain
 *      starts there. The one allowed exception is theme_override in the DROP_FILE
 *      branch (it styles the dropped PDF), which is why DROP_FILE keeps it. The
 *      reverse check reads main.js's optionsObj literal: a key the form writes
 *      for EVERY type must be read by some non-EMAIL path, or be pruned. A
 *      synthetic reader injected into the DB_DUMP branch proves the scan can fail.
 *   B. The helper, lifted from manage_schedule.php by the tokenizer.
 *   C. The SHIPPED endpoint. manage_schedule.php is read and five lines are
 *      rewritten (four requires, the CSRF guard, the Content-Type header) plus
 *      every `exit;` -> a throw; each rewrite is COUNTED and asserted. Each
 *      scenario runs in its own php.exe (schedprune_child.php) against a fake
 *      database that records every statement; a statement the fake does not
 *      recognise fails the scenario instead of quietly answering "no rows".
 *
 * TARGETED MUTANTS always run: six edits to the file under test, each of which
 * must be killed by the assertion that names it.
 *
 *   php.exe -n -d memory_limit=1024M test_schedprune.php --app=<webroot> [--manage=<file>] [--template=<file>] [--control]
 *
 * --manage overrides the manage_schedule.php under test (a staged copy); the
 * runner and main.js still come from --app. --control adds one deliberately
 * false assertion, which MUST report FAIL.
 *
 * EXPECTED TAIL: "PASS 214   FAIL 0" (--control: FAIL 1; the pre-fix
 * manage_schedule.php: FAIL 27). Any other tail is a failure, a smaller pass
 * count included - a harness error scores zero, it does not report FAIL.
 * --dump prints Part A's per-key classification table.
 */

$APP = null; $MANAGE = null; $CONTROL = false; $DUMP = false; $TEMPLATE = null;
foreach ($argv as $a) {
